  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1121 AM CDT Tue Jul 28 2026\n\nThe region remains on the northeastern edge of a strong upper-\nlevel ridge through Wednesday. This will support highs in the lower\n90s across much of the area, with middle or upper 90s possible \nin portions of western Minnesota Wednesday. Lower dew points should\nkeep conditions less oppressive than earlier this week. An \nisolated afternoon shower or thunderstorm cannot be ruled out \nnear a weak boundary across southwest Minnesota Tuesday, \nshifting toward east-central Minnesota and northwest Wisconsin \nWednesday. Coverage should be limited, and widespread severe \nweather is not expected. Wildfire smoke from the Pacific \nNorthwest will periodically produce hazy or filtered sunshine. \nMost of this smoke is expected to remain above the surface, \nlimiting air-quality impacts.\n\nThursday through Friday Night \u2014 \nA stronger disturbance and associated surface low will move \ninto the Upper Midwest Thursday and Friday. Increasing southerly\nflow ahead of this system will return deeper moisture to \nMinnesota and Wisconsin, with thunderstorms becoming more \nnumerous from west to east. Scattered storms may develop across \nwestern Minnesota Thursday afternoon before expanding eastward \nThursday night. The Storm Prediction Center currently has a \nLevel 1 of 5 risk for severe thunderstorms across portions of \nthe Upper Mississippi Valley. Damaging wind gusts appear to be \nthe primary severe- weather hazard, especially if storms \norganize into one or more clusters. Large hail cannot be ruled \nout with the strongest storms, but limited wind shear may keep \nthe overall severe threat from becoming widespread. Heavy \nrainfall may become the more widespread concern Thursday night \ninto Friday. Precipitable water values will be anomalously \nhigh, and slow-moving training storms may repeatedly impact the \nsame locations. A good deal of uncertainty still exists with \nthe favored placement of the frontal boundary & corridor of \nheaviest rain, although current guidance places the strongest \nsignal from Iowa into southeast Minnesota and southwest \nWisconsin. High-resolution model guidance highlights the \npotential for rainfall amounts of 3-4\" wherever the main swath \nof thunderstorms occurs, & localized amounts near 6\" appear \npossible as well. Our dry conditions over the last two weeks \nshould mitigate the flash flood threat somewhat, but urban \nflooding is a risk as well as small stream/creek flooding rural \nareas.\n\nThe late- week system should gradually move east and south \nSaturday. Showers may linger into Saturday morning, particularly\nacross Wisconsin, before drier air becomes more established. \nLess- organized waves of showers and storms may continue across \nnorthern Minnesota and northern Wisconsin, but southern portions\nof both states have a better chance of remaining dry Sunday. \nTemperatures should settle closer to late- July normals behind \nthe system, with highs mainly in the lower to middle 80s. Warmer\nconditions may return early next week as the ridge begins \nexpanding eastward again.",
